A||||9||Seanh||Oil Additives & Internal Engine Cleaning||||5||
Z||000000||Seanh||11-26-2000||08:11 PM||||I was just wondering if there are any oil additives I should get or ones I should stay away from? I use the Slick-50 treatment at the reccomened intervals, but I was thinking of the ones that you put in about 200 miles before your oil change. Do they really do any good? Are they worth the $15? I heard you can use ATF? How much do you put in, and how far before an oil change should I put it in? I also heard of flushing the engine with Karosine(sp?) between oil changes, is this safe to do? <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/transsig.gif">  <BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>"If you smoke after sex, you're doing it way to fast".</I>||||reg||5
Z||000001||strdream||11-26-2000||09:25 PM||||Most of the oil additives are "best" in the eyes of the sales person.  If you are trying to solve a particular problem than you could look at products that are proven to help those conditions.  But to just thro in an additive for the sake of doing it is likly to be a waste of money.  The good quality oils on the market today have all of the additives you need for normal conditoins.  <BR>There is just is no substitute for timely maintainance with quality products.  My Olds 3800 has 266,000 mi on it with no build up.<BR> The ATF and karosine treatments are aimed at sludge build up.  If you don't have sludge build up than don't bother. <BR> A word of caution!<BR>  If the sludge brakes up rapidly it could go thru the oil passages and produce a blockage or plug up the oil filter.<P>Ross    ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||11-26-2000||10:03 PM||||Slick 50 is snake oil and not recommended.  Do a search on the forum for that topic.  There are links to the horror stories of  the ineffectiveness of most all the oil additives.  The teflon particle additives were the worst.  The particles settle out, when the car is off, then clog internal oil lines when pumped back through.  Quaker has signed a consent order on how it advertises Slick 50.<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000003||Seanh||11-26-2000||11:44 PM||||The Fiero has little to no build up because I change the oil on schedule all the time. The owner before me didnt take good car of it, but appently he changed the oil often enough. Slick 50 is bad??? I am glad I mentioned it now. I herd me engine running beofore I added it, and then again after I added it, and I swear up and down it runs quieter. I dont know if S-50 made it run quieter in a safe way, but it does run quieter. The reason I ask about the ATF is for my winter beater. Has 266K and is puffing oil out the breather on the oil cap and plugging the filter in a months time. I was told by Ford (of course their answer to everything is put a new engine in it) that the oil passages were clogged up and would need to be cleaned out by removing the valve covers. I had them do it once about 4 years ago, and now it is clogging up again. I dont change oil all that often because it goes through it so fast theres really no need. I do keep the filter changed though. Should I pour some AFT in there and change the oil every 2000 miles until it comes out smooth? Are any of these additives for "oil burning engines" any good? This winter truck burns more oil then it does gas usally.||||reg||1
Z||000004||TheFiero64||11-27-2000||10:36 AM||||look at this thread <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/006877.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/006877.html</A> <P>------------------<BR>88 Coupe V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||3
Z||000005||Richard Parnell||11-27-2000||12:48 PM||||Use Marvel Mystery Oil.  Do a search re:.||||reg||12
Z||000006||theogre||11-27-2000||08:57 PM||||DuPont has maintained that Teflon will not work as advertised in engine aditives by anyone. Unless something has changed very recently DuPont will not make or endorse any such product. <P>DuPont would prefer not to sell teflon to these people and infact tried to restrict sales of it in this application. The makers of Slick50 and others that use Teflon sued DuPont and won a court order to release the stuff to them.<P>DuPont has agressivly banned the use of the DuPont and Teflon logos on these products. This is why they all use the generic term PTFE to describe the aditive. <P>I recently saw one new use of it on a product at K-Mart. I'm presently checking to see if this is a legal DuPont License to display the logo or what. If it's licensed then it would be the first I know of.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Smokey||11-27-2000||09:21 PM||||A friend of mine has a 5.0 stang. Every time he's changed his oil since he's owned the car...he filled it up with 5 quarts of ATF and let it run for 5 minutes before he put new oil in it. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Indiana_resto_guy||11-27-2000||09:57 PM||||Seanh,<P>The thing that you must remember with the choices you've mentioned, DON'T RUN THE ENGINE AT NORMAL OPERATING RPM'S!<BR>Those items do not have the lubricating requirements for long operations or higher RPM's and is not recommended you run the engine for more than 5 minutes.<BR>If that is the route you choose, don't run the engine any faster than idle and do it with a cool engine.<BR>What I use before an oil change is B-12 CHEMTOOL by Berryman (available at Wal-Mart). Use just before oil change following directions on can. This can also be used in your gas and it cleans check valves, fuel pumps, injectors and combustion chambers. Not to mention it's moisture removing capabilities. It does an excelent job.<BR>Another choice is to use Rislone oil treatment, this is run with the new oil as a substitute for a quart at oil change and is a cleaner.<BR>Is your PVC valve in good working order?<p>[This message has been edited by Indiana_resto_guy (edited 11-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||strdream||11-28-2000||08:58 AM||||Your Ford is a differant story.  The sludge is made of carbon,  carbon is a byproduct of combustion.  The only way it can get into the cranck case is past the rings.  Otherwise known as blow-by.  Running short trips the engine does not get fully wormed up.  The carbon vapor condences on the colder surfaces.  The right way to remody this is to replace worn parts.  ||||reg||1
